  "format": "call-of-slop-surfaces/1",
  "about": "The shared material library (docs/render/MATERIALS.md). One row is one material: parts, props and extrusions hold its number, never a copy, and the GPU holds one table, so changing a material here changes everything made of it. Rows keep their order: the first seven are the texture layers of the old Material enum (Grid to Dirt, drawn the classic way) and must stay; new rows go at the end. rough: 0 mirror, 1 chalk. metal: 0 paint, stone, wood; 1 bare metal. tint: how much of the box's own colour is kept (1: all of it; below 1 it leans to 'colour'). pattern: none, brushed, speckle, grain, weave, tread, panel; scale: repeats a metre; amount: how strong. wear: how much edges rub through to 'wearColour'. rust: how much a hurt part rusts or chars (0 none). painted: the row a painted box uses instead. The 'rules' give every part box its material: the first rule that fits wins, with 'role', 'material' (the part's), 'folder' (a start of its folder), 'name' (in its name), 'glass', 'glow', 'tyre' (a box that takes no paint on a wheel), 'hue' ([from, to] degrees, with saturation above 0.15) and 'dark' (below this brightness). A part file can name one with \"finish\" on a box or the whole part. 'bulk' does the same for an extrusion by its bulk material. 'props' maps a prop part's texture material to a row.",
